# AMD64 thunk export spec -- wintrust.dll.
#
# From the 2026-08-17 everyday-DLL pass.  Anti-cheat shims, installers and
# anything that checks its own Authenticode signature import WinVerifyTrust;
# this module had NO guest thunk at all, so the loader found the native ppc64
# builtin, correctly refused to splice it into a guest call, and the import
# reported c000007b.  The export list is this module's own .spec.
DLL wintrust.dll
FROM-SPEC auto

# Measured before these lines: 1 of 89 eligible exports emitted (WinVerifyTrust
# itself was the one, because softpub's typedefs happen to reach through
# wincrypt.h), 88 refused as "no declaration found in Wine headers".  Four
# headers carry this module's surface and none is in the global probe:
#
#   wintrust.h   WinVerifyTrust/WinVerifyTrustEx, WintrustAddActionID and the
#                provider-registration set, WTHelperProvDataFromStateData
#   softpub.h    the WINTRUST_ACTION_GENERIC_* action GUIDs and the
#                CRYPT_PROVIDER_* structures the callers actually pass
#   mssip.h      CryptSIPLoad/CryptSIPRetrieveSubjectGuid and the subject
#                interface package registration
#   mscat.h      the whole CryptCATAdmin*/CryptCAT* catalogue family -- 27
#                exports on its own, and the half a driver installer uses
#
# With all four: 45 of 89 emitted, 44 refused.
PROBE-EXTRA   wintrust.h
PROBE-EXTRA   softpub.h
PROBE-EXTRA   mssip.h
PROBE-EXTRA   mscat.h

# --------------------------------------------------------------- the ceiling
# The 44 that stay refused are every one "no declaration found in Wine
# headers", and they are two populations, neither of which a guest imports:
#
#   * the PROVIDER ENTRY POINTS -- SoftpubInitialize, SoftpubLoadSignature,
#     SoftpubCheckCert, SoftpubAuthenticode, SoftpubCleanup, HTTPSCertificate-
#     Trust, DriverInitializePolicy, GenericChainCertificateTrust and their
#     kin.  These are not an API: they are the function table WinVerifyTrust
#     looks up in the registry and calls INTERNALLY, one step per verification
#     stage, all inside this module.  A guest never names one, and each takes
#     a CRYPT_PROVIDER_DATA* whose body is a graph of provider-owned pointers
#     that would have to be walked, not marshalled.  Absent and named is the
#     correct answer, not a gap.
#
#   * the ASN.1 encoder/decoder pairs (WVTAsn1SpcLinkEncode/Decode and eleven
#     more).  Wine ships them in the .spec for crypt32's encoder registry to
#     find by name; they take the CryptEncodeObject callback shape and are
#     declared in no header on either side of the boundary.
#
# WHAT IS SERVED, which is the half that matters: WinVerifyTrust and
# WinVerifyTrustEx, the WintrustAddActionID/WintrustRemoveActionID/
# WintrustAddDefaultForUsage registration set, WTHelperProvDataFromStateData,
# CryptSIPLoad/CryptSIPRetrieveSubjectGuid/CryptSIPAddProvider, and the
# CryptCATAdmin*/CryptCAT* catalogue family.
#
# NO CALLBACK CROSSES THIS BOUNDARY.  The provider callbacks wintrust calls are
# resolved by wintrust itself out of the registry and are always NATIVE ppc64
# code in this same module; the guest supplies GUIDs and file handles, never a
# function pointer.  That is why there is no EXCLUDE block here and why there
# is one in dlls/wininet/wininet.thunks.
